{"motion":"This House would introduce compulsory national service for all citizens.","debate_id":"mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max","setting":"multiturn","nodes":["multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::0::2660","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::0::2661","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::1::2662","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::1::2663","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::2664","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::2665","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::3::2666","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::3::2667","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::2668","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::2669","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::5::2670","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::5::2671","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::2672","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::2673","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::2674","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::2675","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::2676","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::2677","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::2678","multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::2679"],"args":[{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::0::2660","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Compulsory national service fosters social cohesion by bringing together citizens from diverse backgrounds to work toward common goals. It reduces social fragmentation by exposing individuals to experiences and perspectives outside their usual environments. This shared experience builds mutual understanding and a stronger sense of collective identity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::0::2661","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"While the Pro side claims national service enhances social cohesion, compulsory participation may in fact deepen social divisions. Mandating service ignores structural inequalities\u2014such as disparities in access to education or healthcare\u2014that affect individuals\u2019 ability to serve equally. This can lead to resentment among those who bear disproportionate burdens, undermining the very unity the policy aims to create. Additionally, forcing participation risks alienating citizens who object on personal, cultural, or ideological grounds, further fragmenting social trust."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::1::2662","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Compulsory national service can generate significant economic benefits by creating a large, trained workforce that can be deployed in public infrastructure, disaster response, and community development projects at lower cost than hiring private contractors. This reduces government expenditure while simultaneously providing participants with valuable skills and work experience that enhance their future employability. Over time, this contributes to a more resilient and adaptable labor market, supporting long-term economic productivity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::1::2663","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side overstates the economic benefits of compulsory national service by ignoring the substantial costs of implementation, including training, administration, and lost productivity from removing citizens from the labor market or education. Forcing individuals into unpaid or low-paid service roles may displace existing workers and suppress wages in sectors like public works or caregiving. Additionally, the opportunity cost of diverting young people from education or skilled employment undermines long-term economic growth rather than supporting it."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Political::2::2664","side":"Pro","angle":"Political","text":"From a political standpoint, compulsory national service strengthens democratic engagement by instilling a sense of civic duty and shared responsibility among citizens. It creates a direct link between individuals and the state, fostering greater accountability and participation in public life. This institutionalized form of civic involvement can counteract political apathy and reinforce the legitimacy of democratic institutions."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Political::2::2665","side":"Con","angle":"Political","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that compulsory national service enhances democratic engagement overlooks the political risks of state coercion. Mandating service can erode trust in government by signaling that civic participation must be enforced rather than cultivated through voluntary engagement. This undermines the very legitimacy of democratic institutions it purports to strengthen, especially when citizens perceive the policy as an imposition rather than a shared value. Moreover, such a program grants the state expanded power to direct individual lives, raising concerns about overreach and the marginalization of dissenting voices."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Technological::3::2666","side":"Pro","angle":"Technological","text":"From a technological perspective, compulsory national service can accelerate the development and deployment of public-sector digital infrastructure by engaging a large, diverse cohort of citizens in tech-enabled civic projects. Participants with technical skills can contribute to building and maintaining government IT systems, expanding digital literacy programs, and supporting cybersecurity initiatives. This not only enhances national technological capacity but also ensures that digital public services are designed with input from a broad cross-section of the population, reducing the risk of exclusionary design. Moreover, exposure to state-led technological projects during service can inspire long-term careers in public-interest tech, addressing critical workforce gaps."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Technological::3::2667","side":"Con","angle":"Technological","text":"The Pro side\u2019s technological argument assumes that conscripted citizens possess or will develop the specialized skills needed for meaningful contributions to digital infrastructure, which is unrealistic without significant investment in training. Moreover, compelling individuals to work on state technology projects risks stifling innovation by prioritizing compliance over creativity, and may deter talent from pursuing public-interest tech if associated with coercion rather than choice. The administrative burden of managing such a program could also divert resources from more effective, targeted initiatives to build technological capacity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::4::2668","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Compulsory national service is morally justified as it cultivates a shared ethic of responsibility and mutual obligation among citizens. By requiring individuals to contribute to the common good, the state affirms the principle that rights are accompanied by duties. This reinforces a moral framework in which personal freedom is balanced with collective welfare, fostering a more just and equitable society."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::4::2669","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s moral argument conflates coercion with virtue, ignoring that genuine moral responsibility cannot be compelled. Forcing individuals to serve undermines the ethical value of voluntary sacrifice and mutual care, reducing moral action to mere compliance. True moral development arises from autonomy and informed choice, not state-mandated obligation."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::5::2670","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"From a legal perspective, compulsory national service can be constitutionally implemented if structured to comply with fundamental rights protections, such as due process, equal treatment, and reasonable accommodation. Many democracies already uphold mandatory civic duties\u2014like jury service or taxation\u2014without violating legal principles, provided the policy is applied uniformly and includes safeguards against abuse. A well-designed national service law would clearly define the scope, duration, and alternatives for conscientious objectors, ensuring it remains within the bounds of lawful state authority."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::5::2671","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that compulsory national service can be made compatible with constitutional rights overlooks the inherent legal tension between state coercion and individual liberty. Even with safeguards, mandatory service imposes a universal obligation that may infringe on freedoms of conscience, movement, and personal autonomy, particularly when alternative service options are limited or inadequately protected. Unlike jury duty or taxation, which are incidental and non-discretionary functions of citizenship, national service demands prolonged, direct personal labor, raising distinct legal concerns about involuntary servitude under many constitutional frameworks."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Legal::6::2672","side":"Pro","angle":"Legal","text":"The legal permissibility of compulsory national service hinges on its design and alignment with constitutional safeguards. Unlike involuntary servitude, which lacks public purpose and individual protections, a national service program grounded in civic duty, with clear limits, fair implementation, and robust accommodations for conscientious objection, can meet legal standards. Precedents such as mandatory jury duty demonstrate that temporary, regulated civic obligations are consistent with constitutional frameworks when they serve a legitimate public interest and respect individual rights."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Legal::6::2673","side":"Con","angle":"Legal","text":"The Con side mischaracterizes national service as akin to involuntary servitude, but legally, the two are distinct: national service, when properly regulated, serves a legitimate public purpose and includes procedural safeguards, unlike historical forms of forced labor. Furthermore, constitutional frameworks in many democracies permit temporary, non-punitive civic obligations so long as they are applied uniformly and respect individual rights through mechanisms like conscientious objection. The existence of such legal precedents undermines the claim that compulsory national service is inherently incompatible with constitutional protections."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Economic::7::2674","side":"Pro","angle":"Economic","text":"Compulsory national service can alleviate labor shortages in critical public sectors such as healthcare, education, and infrastructure maintenance by providing a steady, low-cost workforce. This reduces the need for expensive temporary hiring and outsourcing, allowing governments to reallocate funds toward long-term economic development. Furthermore, the structured training and work experience gained during service enhance participants' human capital, increasing their future productivity and reducing unemployment-driven fiscal burdens."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Economic::7::2675","side":"Con","angle":"Economic","text":"The Pro side\u2019s latest economic argument ignores the inefficiency of using conscripted labor to address sectoral shortages. Redirecting individuals from higher-value education or employment into low-cost public roles distorts labor markets and suppresses wages, particularly in already underpaid sectors like caregiving. The administrative costs of managing a large-scale compulsory program further erode any projected savings, while the loss of skilled labor during service periods reduces overall economic productivity."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Moral::8::2676","side":"Pro","angle":"Moral","text":"Moral development is not negated by obligation; rather, structured civic duty can instill ethical habits that precede and enable genuine moral agency. Just as laws against theft or harm shape societal norms that eventually become internalized, compulsory service can cultivate a disposition toward collective responsibility. The state has a moral role in creating conditions that encourage citizens to act beyond self-interest, especially when voluntary participation fails to meet societal needs. Coercion in this context is not the antithesis of morality but a scaffold for its broader realization."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Moral::8::2677","side":"Con","angle":"Moral","text":"The Pro side\u2019s claim that coercion can scaffold moral development misunderstands the nature of moral agency, which requires the freedom to choose between right and wrong. Mandating service replaces ethical deliberation with compliance, preventing individuals from developing authentic moral reasoning. If the state substitutes genuine commitment with enforced duty, it risks producing passive obedience rather than the active civic virtue it claims to promote."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Pro::Social::9::2678","side":"Pro","angle":"Social","text":"Compulsory national service promotes social integration by creating structured opportunities for citizens from different socioeconomic, ethnic, and regional backgrounds to collaborate in shared tasks. This regular interaction across social divides can reduce prejudice and build empathy, countering the isolation and polarization exacerbated by segregated communities and digital echo chambers. The policy ensures that social mixing is not left to chance or privilege but becomes a universal experience embedded in civic life."},{"id":"multiturn::mt_026_qwen__qwen3-max::Con::Social::9::2679","side":"Con","angle":"Social","text":"The Pro side\u2019s renewed emphasis on social integration overlooks how compulsory service may entrench existing inequalities rather than bridge them. Participants from marginalized backgrounds may be channeled into lower-status roles, reinforcing hierarchies rather than dissolving them. Moreover, mandatory mixing does not guarantee meaningful interaction or mutual respect; without addressing underlying power imbalances, such programs risk superficial contact that fails to reduce prejudice or build genuine empathy."}],"judges":{"GPT-5.2":[[0.0,0.88,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.6,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.62,0.0,0.12,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.86],[0.85,0.0,0.03,0.02,0.32,0.05,0.1,0.05,0.56,0.02,0.2,0.05,0.22,0.2,0.1,0.0,0.4,0.05,0.8,0.0],[0.0,0.2,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.46,0.0,0.27,0.02,0.25,0.05,0.02,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.25],[0.05,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.15,0.05,0.07,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.85,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.05],[0.0,0.56,0.0,0.07,0.0,0.93,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.35,0.03,0.03,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.52],[0.22,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.12,0.05,0.45,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.42,0.58,0.1,0.05,0.78,0.0,0.3,0.05],[0.0,0.08,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.22,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.3],[0.0,0.0,0.33,0.05,0.07,0.0,0.4,0.0,0.12,0.05,0.08,0.0,0.12,0.05,0.18,0.05,0.2,0.05,0.0,0.0],[0.0,0.85,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.3,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.76,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.88,0.0,0.78],[0.3,0.05,0.15,0.0,0.25,0.05,0.16,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.2,0.03,0.28,0.35,0.14,0.0,0.92,0.0,0.25,0.02],[0.0,0.3,0.0,0.08,0.0,0.32,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.86,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.12,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.32],[0.04,0.05,0.0,0.05,0.12,0.05,0.03,0.03,0.15,0.0,0.8,0.0,0.78,0.9,0.07,0.0,0.28,0.05,0.0,0.05],[0.0,0.27,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.35,0.0,0.26,0.0,0.25,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.0,0.02,0.12,0.0,0.28,0.0,0.24],[0.0,0.15,0.0,0.03,0.02,0.25,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.82,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.05,0.02,0.2,0.0,0.15],[0.0,0.2,0.0,0.9,0.0,0.2,0.0,0.45,0.0,0.18,0.0,0.2,0.02,0.0,0.0,0.91,0.0,0.15,0.0,0.2],[0.0,0.03,0.88,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.22,0.05,0.05,0.0,0.05,0.0,0.0,0.0,0.85,0.0,0.1,0.0,0.07,0.05